module display_synth.pos_1

// THE MEMBERWISE `@display` SYNTHESIZED FROM THE GATE (D422 section 4, D109 as
// amended 2026-09-18): a record that opted in with `#impl(Display)` and wrote
// no method of its own prints the COMPACT form, without field names.
//
// WHY THE VALUES ARE 1 AND 2 AND NOT SOMETHING MEMORABLE. The whole point of
// this fixture is the SHAPE of the output -- the parentheses, the comma AND the
// space, the absence of field names -- so the numbers are deliberately dull and
// the separator is what carries the assertion. `Point(1, 2)` differs from the
// named form `Point { x: 1, y: 2 }` in five bytes, and a differential comparing
// stdout byte for byte is what notices all five.
//
// WHERE THE SEPARATOR COMES FROM, said plainly because the norm does not say
// it: D422 gives ONE sample and never states whether the space after the comma
// is required. The oracle writes `", "` as its own literal, and the oracle is
// the fixed point of BEHAVIOUR -- same stdout, same exit code. That is the only
// thing it may be read as; its DESIGN is not a donor (P25), and this fixture
// takes only the bytes.
//
// THE FIXTURE EXISTS BECAUSE THE CORPUS DOES NOT COVER THIS. Measured
// 2026-09-18: after the synthesis landed, the full corpus run returned numbers
// identical to the run before it -- 60/6/48/2, behaviour 58 of 60 -- because no
// corpus file prints a gated record in a slot. A green corpus after a new
// capability reads as "the capability works", and that reading would have been
// wrong. This file is what makes the capability visible to a MECHANISM instead
// of to the window that wrote it.

#impl(Display)
type Point {
    x int
    y int
}

fn main() {
    ro p = Point{ x: 1, y: 2 }
    println("${p}")
}
